# Build output in examples. `npm run build` regenerates all of dist/, so
# tracking any of it makes the tree dirty after a build -- and the AS end-to-end
# test in engine/as_workflow_e2e_test.go RUNS that build, so `go test ./engine/`
# was enough to do it.
#
# This rule used to be `examples/*/dist/*.wasm`, which covered the artifact the
# READMEs actually name (`cleat deploy as-workflow dist/workflow.stamped.wasm`)
# and left AssemblyScript's generated glue tracked. Four files, workflow.js and
# workflow.d.ts in two examples, none of them read by anything:
#
#   grep -rn "as-workflow/dist" --include="*.go" --include="*.md" .   # no hits
#
# They were also STALE -- the committed workflow.js disagreed with what the
# pinned toolchain produces, and the build is deterministic (two runs
# byte-identical), so the difference was age, not nondeterminism. A committed
# artifact that nothing reads and that does not match its own lockfile's output
# is worse than an absent one: a reader takes it for the real thing.
#
# The same lesson is recorded 100 lines below for the asworkflow test fixture,
# where only dist/workflow.wasm is a fixture and the stamped copy is ignored
# "because left untracked it makes the tree dirty after any asworkflow build".
# This is that case again, one directory over.
examples/*/dist/

# Cloned source repos (not our work product)
ports-round2/dbos-demo-apps/
ports-round2/examples/
ports-round2/samples-go/
ports-round2/samples-python/

# Rust build artifacts in ports
ports-round2/*/target/
ports-round2/*/Cargo.lock

# Go sum files in ports
ports-round2/*/go.sum

# Gradle build in ports
ports-round2/*/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
ports-round2/*/.gradle/
ports-round2/*/build/

# Benchmark baseline files (CI artifacts, not committed)
.benchmarks/
.claude/worktrees/
/cleatctl

.go-cache/

# Java build artifacts
crates/cleat-java/.gradle/
crates/cleat-java/build/
examples/*/node_modules/

# Gradle build artifacts anywhere (these were being swept into commits from
# examples/ because the rules above were path-specific).
.gradle/
**/.gradle/
examples/*/build/

# The plugin-harness Java workflow is compiled by TestPluginCalls_Wasm_Java on
# every run, so everything under its build/ is scratch. The one artifact that
# is a fixture -- the .wasm that wasm/import_section_test.go and
# cmd/cleat-worker/backend_routing_test.go read -- lives in prebuilt/ instead,
# outside any directory Gradle owns.
tests/plugin-harness/testdata/javaworkflow/build/

# Same for the Java host-call fixture, which TestHostCallsJava rebuilds on
# every run. Nothing under build/ is a fixture here -- the harness reads the
# .wasm out of the -o directory, never from the source tree -- so unlike
# javaworkflow there is no prebuilt/ counterpart to keep. Added with the
# directory rather than after the first dirty `git status`, which is how
# §3.305 and §3.308 both started.
tests/plugin-harness/testdata/hostcallsjava/build/

# CI test-report artifacts (go test -json | tee ...), written into the repo
# root by the workflow steps that check skip budgets.
*-report.json
tier-gate.log

# scripts/tier2-gate.sh output
tier2-gate.log
tier2-gate.json
.tier2-verdict

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Untracked 2026-08-09 (hotfix/rotate-committed-credential).
#
# Every path below was ALREADY matched by a rule in this file -- `node_modules/`
# at line 66 covers all 445 node_modules entries, `/cleat-worker` at line 13
# covers the root binary. They stayed tracked anyway, because .gitignore does
# not apply to a path git is already tracking: the rule was added after the
# file, and was a silent no-op from the day it landed.
#
# So the rules below are not the fix. `git rm --cached` was the fix; these
# exist to stop the same files coming back. If you add an ignore rule for
# something that is already committed, `git rm --cached` it in the same commit
# or you have changed nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Agent/orchestration scratch state. Not referenced by Makefile, CI, or go.mod
# (verified: `git grep -l task_state -- Makefile .github/workflows` is empty).
clew-agent.json
clew-executor.plugin
task_state/
plans/
prompts/
projects/
session-*.json

# Build products. 332 MB of these were tracked in HEAD, including binaries
# built on three different machines (Mach-O arm64 and ELF x86-64 side by side).
bin/
cmd/cleat-worker/cleat-worker
/durable-worker
/durable-bench

# Unreferenced root-level demos carrying `//go:build ignore`, so `go build ./...`
# never saw them and nothing noticed they were dead.
/ast_transform_demo.go
/ast_transform_demo_v2.go

# The whole of the AS test fixture's dist/ is build output now, and none of it
# is tracked. This rule used to name only dist/workflow.stamped.wasm, on the
# reasoning that "only dist/workflow.wasm is a fixture" -- true about which file
# has readers, wrong about which files the build writes. `cleat build --target
# assemblyscript` regenerates dist/workflow.wasm itself, so
# TestPluginCalls_Wasm_AS rewrote the tracked fixture on every run and left the
# tree dirty. Measured 2026-09-04: 13369 bytes committed, 13672 after one run,
# and byte-identical across two runs -- so the difference was age, not
# nondeterminism, the same distinction drawn 100 lines above for examples/.
#
# The fixture the two tests read now lives in ../prebuilt/, out of the build's
# reach, exactly as javaworkflow/prebuilt/ already did for the same reason.
# workflow.js and workflow.d.ts are AssemblyScript's generated glue with no
# reader (`grep -rn 'dist/workflow\.\(js\|d\.ts\)' --include='*.go'
# --include='*.md' --exclude-dir=node_modules .` finds none) and are untracked
# rather than moved.
tests/plugin-harness/testdata/asworkflow/dist/

# goreleaser output. Produced by `goreleaser build --snapshot`, which the
# Release Dry Run CI job runs and which is the documented way to reproduce a
# release build locally (docs/project/release-process.md).
dist/
